## Is glaucoma a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. If you are certified as **sight impaired** or **severely sight impaired**, you are automatically treated as disabled.

## At work

Reasonable adjustments could include larger screens, magnification software, good lighting, or changes to driving duties. **Access to Work** can pay for equipment and support.

## Benefits

Glaucoma that is well controlled does not usually affect daily life. If you have lost a lot of sight:

PIP is based on how your sight affects you on most days. Sight loss can affect activities such as:

- **reading**, if you cannot read normal size print even with glasses
- **preparing food**, if you cannot safely use a cooker or chop food
- **planning and following journeys**, if you need someone with you, a guide dog or a cane on unfamiliar routes
- **managing medicines**, if you cannot read labels
- **making budgeting decisions**, if you need help reading bills and letters

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead of PIP. It is for people who need help with personal care or supervision.

## Registering as sight impaired

If your sight is badly affected, an eye specialist can sign a **Certificate of Vision Impairment (CVI)** so you can register as **sight impaired** or **severely sight impaired** with your council. This can open the door to:

- help from your council's sensory team, such as mobility training and equipment
- a [Blue Badge](/support/travel/blue-badge/), automatically if you are severely sight impaired
- a [Disabled Persons Railcard](/support/travel/disabled-persons-railcard/) and a free [bus pass](/support/travel/bus-passes/)
- **Blind Person's Allowance**, which raises your tax-free income, if you are severely sight impaired
- **50% off** your TV licence, if you are severely sight impaired

Registering is voluntary, and you can get some help without it.

## Free sight tests

NHS sight tests are free if you have glaucoma. You may also get help with glasses. See [help with eye care costs](/support/health-costs/eye-care/).
